      {
        "trackId": "nh_auto_nonconviction",
        "affectedElement": "participant_instructions",
        "question": "What a participant does when the automatic annulment does not happen. RSA 651:5 provides no express enforcement route, and the statute was read in full at gc.nh.gov on 2026-08-06 without finding one."
      },
      {
        "trackId": "nh_auto_nonconviction",
        "affectedElement": "eligibility_branch",
        "question": "Whether \"not prosecuted\" covers a nolle prosequi in New Hampshire practice. The statute uses \"dismissed or not prosecuted\" without defining either."
      },
      {
        "trackId": "nh_auto_nonconviction",
        "affectedElement": "eligibility_branch",
        "question": "Whether RSA 651:5, VI can block an automatic annulment where the person has other convictions. Paragraph VI is written around petitions and convictions and does not on its face gate the automatic route."
      },
      {
        "trackId": "nh_auto_vacated",
        "affectedElement": "eligibility_branch",
        "question": "Whether \"disposed of on or after January 1, 2019\" in RSA 651:5, II-a(b) refers to the original disposition or to the vacatur. This decides whether an old case with a recent vacatur is automatic or needs a petition, and the subparagraph was read at gc.nh.gov on 2026-08-06 without resolving it."
      },
      {
        "trackId": "nh_auto_vacated",
        "affectedElement": "participant_instructions",
        "question": "Whether the annulment is immediate on vacatur. Subparagraph (b) states no thirty-day clock and no appeal trigger, unlike subparagraph (a)."
      }
    ],
